---
import { Image } from "astro:assets"
import Container from "@components/Container.astro";
import Layout from "@layouts/Layout.astro";
import ImagePopout from "@components/ImagePopout.astro";
import StaticGraphic from "@components/StaticGraphic.astro";
import MultiCol from "@components/MultiCol.astro";
import Gap from "@components/Gap.astro";
import Nav from "@components/Nav.astro"
import Footer from "@components/Footer.astro"

import Thumb from './thumbnail-bg.png';
import ThumbText from './thumbnail-text.png';
import BrandGuide from './brand-guide.pdf';
import NPEsportsI1 from './npesports-iteration1.png';
import NPEsportsI2 from './npesports-iteration2.png';
import NPEsportsLogo from './npesports-logo.png';
import NPEsportsIcon from './npesports-icon.png';
import UAarena from './ualbany-arena.jpg';
import UAstream from './ualbany-stream.png';
import Gallery from '@assets/images/galleryText.png';
import GalBG1 from "@assets/images/gallery1.png"
import GalBG2 from "@assets/images/gallery2.png"

import SkipLink from "@components/SkipLink.astro";

---

<Layout title= "Collegiate Design">
  <Nav hLink="../../" />
  <div id="gallery">
    <ImagePopout 
      image={Gallery} 
      alt="Opener for the image gallery" 
      w="210px" 
      galleryID="1"
    />
    <StaticGraphic 
      i1={GalBG1} 
      i2={GalBG2} 
      b="70px" 
      r="80px" 
      w="90px"
    />
  </div>

  <div class="content-contain">
    <ImagePopout image={Thumb} alt="Event Design Thumbnail" w="100%" galleryID="2"/>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <h1 class="header">Collegiate Design</h1>
      <p>
        While a lot of Esports’ design comes from grassroots communitiesor big team-centered organizations,
        it’s also important to talk about where people often start their Esports journeys, the collegiate scene!
        The reason I talk about this as its own section is that there are a lot of unique challenges faced
        when designing for collegiate Esports that are similar to how the other two case studies provide unique challenges.
      </p>
    </div>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>Talking with a Director</h2>
        <p>
          Considering that collegiate Esports is often many people’s first entryway into the Esports scene,
          it’s important to use clever and strong design to help the onboard of potential students.
          But what you may not expect is that designing in the collegiate scene often results in unique challenges you may not expect.
          I have my own experience designing for this field but instead of hearing it from me why don't I introduce you to John Macone,
          current director of Esports at UAlbany. He has been working for Esports and even done graphic design for several school’s
          programs for over a decade! I interviewed him on his experience designing for the collegiate scene and beyond.
          So why not hear about these challenges from him in the video below:
        </p>
      </div>
      <iframe 
        width="1080"
        height="720"
        src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/HqMFFRho_ik"
        class="yt-video"
        title="Interview with John Macone - Esports Visualized" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen>
      </iframe>

      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          From this interview along with my own experience I could see similar issues often popping up.
          Oftentimes Esports is never given a direct label by colleges and this has led to the treatment of it being
          very dependent on the college it is located at. This means that Esports can be treated anywhere from a department
          of the school to a student club, and this drastically affects the liberties that designers for these spaces have.
          Most schools have some defined format for how their sports obtain their branding,
          typically in the form of their marketing department making it for them. With Esports though it is quite the opposite,
          leaving most schools/programs out to dry to form their own branding.
          On top of that most schools have direct restrictions on how the student body can use their logos, fonts, and even potentially colors.
          This leaves designers with very few options when trying to make branding for an Esports program that is intended to directly represent their school. 
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>Collegiate Restrictions</h2>
        <p>
          But contrary to what it seems, these restrictions have served to give Esports a more unique and distinct brand style in the collegiate scene!
          Let's start by showing an example of school-backed programs that deal with these restrictions,
          as talked about in the interview I’d like to highlight University at Albany’s program. With UAlbany their logo for Esports is more standard and traditionally athletic.
          A lot of their brand identity comes from what is around the logo. This is visible more so on something like their Twitch stream’s graphics.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={UAstream} alt="Mid Hudson Mayhem's icon logo" w="800px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          Here they take the same logo but apply a brand style more unique to their direct program.
          They make use of a more sketched look with bold stripes to set their program apart from other sports programs that all use the same University branding.
          This allows their program and their in-person arena (visualized below) to stand out as its own distinct organization on campus.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={UAarena} alt="Mid Hudson Mayhem's icon logo" w="700px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>New Paltz Esports</h2>
        <p>
          So then how would somebody work from the ground up to make a college’s collegiate Esports branding with the heavy restrictions
          that would come from an even tighter, student club environment. That is an experience that I had to find out for SUNY New Paltz’s Esports program
          so let me highlight how I tackled these restrictions. Since this is something I worked with the departments at New Paltz for
          I can discuss what the specific restrictions were here. For this project I was required to follow the SUNY New Paltz Style Guide.
          In essence, I could use only New Paltz’s two main fonts, their small color palette, and very restricted use of their athletic logo.
          These are very big restrictions and drastically affect how much liberty I had over the design. You can see here two of my initial iterations I had made before getting news of my restrictions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={NPEsportsI1} alt="Concept for the New Paltz Esports logo" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={NPEsportsI2} alt="Mono color concept for the New Paltz Esports logo" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          This is the initial vision I had for the program with bolder fonts and darker colors to make it stand out more from other New Paltz branding.
          The marketing department saw this being submitted for use and told our organization that we had to follow the restrictions listed above.
          You can see below what I ended up with, still attempting to keep some of the vision of the original and using shape to make it stand out more. 
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={NPEsportsIcon} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={NPEsportsLogo} alt="New Paltz Esports' Full Logo" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          From here, since the logo was more homogenous it was about making the branding stand out from the rest of Athletics.
          I took a similar approach to what we had talked about before and used different shaped lines, stripes, and borders to create a visually distinct style for New Paltz Esports.
          One main thing I implemented across all works was the use of a grain texture to make everything feel more gritty and rough which to me is what competition feels like,
          along with using mostly the Dark Blue as a background where most campus orgs use white as a background when working with the style guide.
          These things to me help give New Paltz Esports its own unique identity at New Paltz.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          Lastly here you can see a short brand guidelines I made for New Paltz Esports in order to visually encompass all of what I had been saying.
          It defines the style of the program and emphasizes this interesting dichotomy. Theres a need to both show to outside organizations and schools
          that we represent New Paltz in the Esports space but at the same time not fall into the trap of blending in with all the other New Paltz organizations
          to the rest of the student body. You can see how I tackled those problems as well as the overall style in the Brand Guidelines below.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <embed 
          src={BrandGuide} 
          type="application/pdf" 
          width="100%" 
          height="800px" 
        />
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
          This experience is one that designers for Esports in collegiate spaces have to deal with all over the country.
          With Esports being very niche and unsupported by colleges it has forced designers in the space to get very creative in standing out from other
          organizations on their campus’ and allowed them to truly branch out and create many unique works.
        </p>
      </div>
  <Footer />
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
</script>

<style>
  .page-contain{
    display: flex;
    align-items: top;
    /* grid-template-columns: 650px 1fr; */
    justify-content: center;
    margin: var(--size-page-margin) 0;
  }
  .content-contain{
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 0 auto;
    gap: 3rem;
    background-color: var(--color-background);
    height: 100%;
    padding: 3rem;
    padding-top: 8rem;
    max-width: 1200px;
    clip-path: polygon(50% 0, 50% 0, 50% 100%, 50% 100%);
    animation: Open 1s both;
  }
  .content-contain p { max-width: 800px; }

  @keyframes Open{
    0%    { clip-path: polygon(50% 0, 50% 0, 50% 100%, 50% 100%); }
    100%  { clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, 100% 100%, 0 100%); }
  }

  .textgroup h2 { color: var(--color-text); font-family: var(--font-headings);}
  .gallery-contain{
    width: 800px;
    padding: 1rem;
    background-color: var(--color-background);
  }

  #gallery{
    position: fixed;
    padding: 1rem;
    right: 40px;
    top: -40px;
    z-index: 1000;
  }
  
  .header{
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    font-size: 60pt;
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  .yt-video {
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    #gallery{
      display: none;
    }
    p{
      font-size: 12pt;
    }
    .content-contain{
      padding: 1rem;
    }
    .header{
      font-size: clamp(24pt, 10vw, 48pt);
    }
  }
</style>